{"about":"be248e4805a64ff3ba6394f7d33da88ef38fcbfe47a3829fdf587463136ac1c3","body":"The argument rests on the premise that universal laws have zero probability, making probability comparisons impossible. However, if all universal theories have a probability of zero, then stating that one has 'higher informational content' does not logically entail it is a 'better' candidate for truth in a way that guides action without assuming the uniformity of nature. The move from 'survived severe tests' to 'logically closer to truth' requires an assumption that the future will resemble the past regarding the severity of tests, which is precisely the principle of induction Popper sought to reject. Without this assumption, the survival of past tests is historically interesting but logically irrelevant to future truth proximity.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The status of zero probability"}